how to create an elevator pitch so in  the world of business an elevator pitch  is usually used for investors we're  trying to get them to sign up with your  company and the idea is you're getting  on the top floor of a building with the  investor and by the time you're on the  elevator you get to the ground floor  you've convinced them that they want to  learn more about your business so this  what's called an elevator pitch and  usually have about a minute to be able  to talk about your company and get  somebody excited the biggest challenge  here is being able to quickly convey to  somebody the value of your business  so that they want to learn more and a  lot of entrepreneurs have a hard time  being able to be concise and use  language that moves people to action and  make some quickly understand what your  company is all about it's not a great  question on YouTube from a reader I are  born or Ranger 89 who is working on the  elevator pitch tries to get it under one  minute and he says I'm worried that I'm  speaking too quickly and that people  won't understand him and how much over a  minute can I go so in most circles the  one-minute limit is not a hard rule the  only difference would be if you're going  to some kind of competition and they're  timing you and you've got to do it in a  minute the challenge though is I still  encourage you to try to get it under a  minute and not by speaking faster  because by speaking faster people aren't  going to understand what you're doing  it's focusing in on not sharing every  little detail about your business but  trying to get them to understand at a  high level what you're all about what  the opportunity is and why they should  care and want to take the next step with  you from there you'll get into more  questions and due diligence and a whole  lengthy process where you can answer  their questions but you have to tease  them and give them just enough  information that they want to do more  business with you I remember when I had  my first company was a software business  and I was trying to get investors I was  thinking about you know who I wanted to  bring on board and I felt like I felt  like I had a great product I had a great  team I was really helping people we had  we had customers and were getting  traction and I thought if I could only  get the investor to sit down with me for  an hour I can explain everything and he  could help me get to the next step  the problem is investors don't have that  kind of time right you have to take what  might take half an hour or an hour and  crunch it down into one minute enough  for them to get excited about what  you're doing to want to contact you and  take it to the next step and usually  what will help make the difference and  after my first business I went into the  VC world so I I saw from the other side  is show that you first have something  but then show that you have some  momentum because the biggest difference  between people who are just pitching an  idea and people who actually get funding  is having some real momentum with your  business okay so you have somebody on  your Board of Advisors or you've  actually made a sale right just having a  little bit of revenue not not a ton a  little bit already separates you from  90% of the other companies or plus okay  you've been in the media you have some  kind of traction somewhere show that  it's not just an idea that people are  genuinely interested and need this thing  don't focus so much on the technology  all the VC cares about is that this  thing can grow so you're in a space that  that is growing and there's a potential  to make money but they don't care about  what the technology does not in the  first meeting they want to see there's  an opportunity that you have momentum  and that you know what you're talking  about  that shouldn't take more than a minute  to get through practice in the VC world  we used to call the grandmother test  does your pitch pass the grandmother  test and that's where it's simple enough  that your grandmother could understand  it remember that investors are not  experts in your industry okay they are  generalists they understand the finances  side really well but in terms of  Industry specific they're a generalist  they know a little bit about a lot of  industries they don't know your business  as well as you do so you need to be able  to create a pitch that is not full of  jargon that's not full of short short  terms or abbreviations something that  makes sense for an outsider like your  grandmother to look at it and understand  what you're doing but I would highly  encourage you to still try to keep it  under a minute and not worry if it's 58  seconds or a minute five most of the  time they won't actually time you but  it's more for your own your own skill  set and for you to be able to get a  powerful result to be able to practice  getting everything down really tightly  just to arouse curiosity and this goes  for everything this isn't just your  elevator pitch this is any communication  you're doing with people it's your pitch  to the media it's your website homepage  it's your sales letter it's all the  marketing copy you use everything you do  you have to be able to quickly get your  point across and use as few words as  possible to have an impact and lead  people to action good luck with your  project if you have more questions let  me know I'm excited for you and I hope  that you will believe for those of you  watching you like the video please give  it a thumbs up it makes you want to do  more for you and let me know what you  have to think leave a question  underneath leave a comment below I'll  make a video response thank you so much  and I'll see you soon
 

